From 7f99e4be2d5f1106c862c1190e2768c9013ab294 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: aydinmrnv <97070767+aydinmrnv@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 11:25:25 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] NVRM: publish which NVDEC/NVENC instances the RM reports

The GPU profile names the newest video class (0xCFB0 / 0xCFB7 on every
consumer Blackwell chip) but not which engine instances a card exposes.
GB203 boards lack NVENC0 (#105) and RM's class tables give GB202 four
NVDEC and four NVENC against one each on GB206 (the tested RTX 5060).
Issue #425 (RTX 5090, no hardware decode) has no log that shows which
instances exist.

nvrm_query_gpu_profile now also issues NV2080_CTRL_CMD_GPU_GET_ENGINES_V2
on the subdevice it already holds, logs the raw engine list, and returns
NVDEC/NVENC instance masks (NVENC3 is engine type 0x3f, not 0x1e).
publishGpuProfile adds nvdec-instances / nvenc-instances to
nvrm-gpu-profile only when the RM answered. A failed query leaves the
rest of the profile unchanged. Diagnostic only: nothing selects an
engine from it.

tools/test_gpu_profile_video_engines.py (pure Python) checks the control
id, list size and engine table against NVIDIA's headers, replays the
mask derivation on the #105 engine list, and has negative controls for
contiguous NVENC numbering and a stale extern "C" declaration.

Not compiled or run on macOS: needs the NVRM kext build and a GB202.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
